# Example for ~/.zshrc.local — machine-local settings and secrets.
#
# Copy to ~/.zshrc.local (NOT into this repo) and chmod 600:
#
#     cp zsh/zshrc.local.example ~/.zshrc.local
#     chmod 600 ~/.zshrc.local
#
# ~/.zshrc.local is sourced last by .zshrc and lives in $HOME, outside the
# repo, so it is never part of a commit. This file — the .example — is tracked
# and must therefore contain no real values.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read this before pasting an AWS key in here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Exporting AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY works, but it is the weakest of the options
# below, for reasons that have nothing to do with git:
#
#   * every process you launch inherits it, including any npm/pip postinstall
#     script and anything that shells out
#   * it appears in `/proc/<pid>/environ`, readable by your own user, so any
#     compromised process can read it
#   * long-lived keys do not rotate and do not expire
#   * it is one careless `env > debug.txt` away from a bug report
#
# Prefer, in order:
#
#   1. AWS SSO / IAM Identity Center  — short-lived, nothing static on disk
#   2. aws-vault                      — keys in the system keyring, never
#                                       plaintext; exports temporary creds
#   3. ~/.aws/credentials + profiles  — still plaintext, but confined to one
#                                       0600 file instead of every process
#   4. exported env vars              — last resort
#
# Only AWS_PROFILE and AWS_REGION belong in a shell rc. Those are not secrets.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. AWS SSO  (best — no long-lived credentials anywhere)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Configure once with:  aws configure sso
# Then log in with:     aws sso login --profile work
#
# export AWS_PROFILE=work
# export AWS_REGION=ap-southeast-1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. aws-vault  (keys encrypted in the system keyring)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Store a key once, then never type or paste it again:
#     aws-vault add work
# Run a command with temporary credentials:
#     aws-vault exec work -- terraform plan
#
# export AWS_VAULT_BACKEND=secret-service   # gnome-keyring / KDE wallet
# export AWS_SESSION_TOKEN_TTL=8h
#
# Handy wrapper: `av work terraform plan`
# av() { aws-vault exec "$1" -- "${@:2}" }


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Profiles in ~/.aws/credentials  (chmod 600)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The profile NAME is not a secret — only the file's contents are. So this
# line is safe even in a committed file:
#
# export AWS_PROFILE=work
# export AWS_REGION=ap-southeast-1
#
# ~/.aws/credentials then holds:
#     [work]
#     aws_access_key_id     = AKIA...
#     aws_secret_access_key = ...


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Raw exports  (last resort — see the warning above)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="REPLACE_ME"
# export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="REPLACE_ME"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Other things that belong here rather than in the repo
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# export GITHUB_TOKEN="..."
# export GITLAB_TOKEN="..."
# export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN="..."
# export TF_VAR_db_password="..."
# export KUBECONFIG="$HOME/.kube/config:$HOME/.kube/work.yaml"
#
# Work proxies, private hosts, machine-specific PATH additions:
# export HTTP_PROXY="http://proxy.internal:3128"
# path=("$HOME/work/bin" $path)
